01 · INTRODUCTION TO JAVA
Why was Java created?
1991 — Green Project
James Gosling and a small team at Sun Microsystems explore software for consumer devices.
1991 — Named "Oak"
The new language is named after an oak tree outside Gosling's office.
1992 — Star7 demo
A handheld prototype (?) shows Oak code running on custom hardware.
1995 — Renamed Java
"Oak" is already trademarked; Java is publicly announced in May 1995.
1996 — JDK 1.0
First official release (January 1996) — Java's mainstream...
The problem
Devices used different chips and operating systems. A C/C++ program had to be recompiled — often rewritten — for each one.

➡️
The idea: Write Once, Run Anywhere
Compile once into portable bytecode. Any device with a Java Virtual Machine (JVM) can run it — no rewrite needed.



How Java Evolved: 30 Years of Steady Upgrades
Java has continuously evolved over the years through regular updates. Each major version introduced new features, improved performance and security, and made programming easier.
Java Versions and Their Features
1. Java 1.0 — January 1996
- JVM (Java Virtual Machine)
- Core libraries
- Applets
- Introduced the concept of "Write Once, Run Anywhere."
2. Java 5 — September 2004
- Generics
- Annotations
- Enums
- Enhanced for loop
3. Java 8 — May 2014
- Lambda expressions
- Stream API
- New Date/Time API
4. Java 11 — September 2018
- First LTS (Long-Term Support) release of the 6-month release era
- Standard HTTP Client
5. Java 17 — September 2021
- Records
- Sealed classes
- Text blocks
- Spring Boot 3 baseline
6. Java 21 — September 2023
- Virtual threads
- Pattern matching for switch
- Record patterns
7. Java 25 — September 2025
- Compact source files
- Scoped values
- Compact object headers
Important Point
LTS — Long-Term Support
LTS (Long-Term Support) is a Java version that receives long-term updates and support.
Remember
Java has continuously evolved by:
- Introducing new features
- Improving performance
- Improving security
- Simplifying programming
In short: Java started with the goal of "Write Once, Run Anywhere" and has continuously improved through new versions and features.



